#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Prints a short story about a candy kingdom.

cat <<'EOF'
The Candy Kingdom of Lollipop Lane

Pink rock-candy dust coats the road to Lollipop Lane; gingerbread spires and spun-caramel chimneys rise through air so sweet your teeth ache before you eat. Mapmakers leave the place blank—no ink stays bright enough.

Princess Marzipan wore a crown of cinnamon spiral and a burden no confection could dissolve. King Nougat the Third had melted in the Great Fondue Eclipse, leaving her a sugar-glass throne and a kingdom one sour season from cracking. Sage Honeycomb, who had stirred the royal cauldrons since Marzipan could walk, stayed when courtiers fled: "Kings melt. Kindness hardens slowly. That is why it outlasts them." Seth, the lane's quiet mapmaker, chalked fresh stripes on the road to the marsh each dawn—lines the fog tried to swallow and he redrew anyway. Twizzle Sticks spun at the gates; Master Crumb muttered over frost lines on the battlements.

Across the Molasses Marsh, the Licorice Duke hungered for the lane. In caverns beneath his dungeons, Lord Pith the Hollow Confectioner—exiled when joy became law for every sweet—fed the duke's jealousy in dreams and distilled spite into syrup for thirty years.

On the night the stars turned bruised plum, Pith turned the crank that unleashed the Sour Fog: gray that tasted of metal and old lemons, draining lollipops to bone-gray sticks and jellybean fields to dull pebbles. Barrel the Herald shouted evacuation routes from the bell tower; Seth passed folded maps hand to hand—routes in honey ink so paper would not crumble, his lantern bobbing like a small moon. Poppy the Popsicle held trembling peppermints and chirped, "Cold hearts crack; warm ones bend."

"The throne is hollow," the duke boomed. "Send me your princess, or I will unmake every sweet thing your father died to protect."

Marzipan took Sage Honeycomb's last honey caramel from the king's jar and crossed the marsh alone. Visions clawed at her—eclipse gold, her father softening at the edges—until Poppy and Sir Fluffington helped her through the worst. Countess Custard hummed the crossing tune from the marsh edge so Marzipan could follow when fog stole the path.

At the licorice drawbridge the duke demanded she kneel. She offered the caramel instead. He struck it into the mud; then he dropped—not in surrender, in shame. "I sent the fog because I could not send myself." The fog lifted because he stopped feeding it.

Pith erupted, hurling distilled envy until gray clawed at Marzipan's boots. She retrieved the caramel. "Eat," she told the duke, "or we both stay gray. Then we end him together." Honey exploded on his tongue; he caught Pith's next vial on his shield. Color rushed back in a wave; Sage Honeycomb's barley-sugar net hardened Pith into bitter quartz mid-curse. The banner unfurled: TRUCE.

That night the feast was vigil turned joyful. Sage ladled caramel for those still shaking; Seth carved the night's story into hard candy at the hall's edge—names, routes, the truce—so later travelers would know the lane had almost been lost and chose otherwise. The duke sat at the foot of the hall until Marzipan pulled his chair beside hers. They danced one turn in silence, two in tears, three in laughter.

Travelers still find the lane by sugar on the wind. They say Marzipan walks the marsh when thunder tastes of sour cherry—not to fight, but to remind the fog what happens when two broken sweets share one caramel. On dark nights, children touch Pith's statue by the drawbridge and swear they feel him still trying, and failing, to turn their laughter sour.

The end.

EOF
